{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit law","requirement":"When a rental property is sold, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and remains responsible for its return; a landlord who willfully withholds a deposit from a tenant receiving government rental assistance may be penalized.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; the penalty applies where the tenant receives State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential tenancies; penalty provision applies to tenants receiving State or federal assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected and enforced by the State entity that made the assistance payments; unlawful use of deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord charges for credit reports and application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge an applicant for the cost of a credit or tenant-screening report and may request a reasonable rental application fee; if the application is denied because of the report, the landlord must give the agency's name, address and phone number.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Landlords and rental agencies screening prospective tenants in New Jersey; the cost of the report may be passed on to the applicant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Prospective tenants screened by New Jersey landlords"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.65,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"LAD source-of-lawful-income protection in renting","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting because of an applicant's source of lawful income, among other protected characteristics.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home and single-sex residences.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Exempt: two-family owner-occupied dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, i.e. YMCA and age-restricted housing, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to substantial fines and penalties, up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction, and government-subsidized rents may also be exempt.","key_value":"30-year exemption from local rent control","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; the exemption runs for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; 30-year period runs from completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances; HUD-subsidized or HUD-insured developments and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act limits on mid-lease and unconscionable rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease: the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent, and the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment.","interaction":"State law requires rent increases to comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on rent increases to cover conversion costs","requirement":"If a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, a landlord may not increase rents to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities remain allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential unit only by the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and only for good cause; a Notice to Quit, and in some cases a Notice to Cease, is required before filing suit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units; no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental dwelling units in New Jersey"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
